# Proof

## Setup and notation

Let $\Omega = (-1,1)^2 \subset \mathbb{R}^2$. The four defining conditions partition $\Omega$ (up to a measure-zero set of boundary lines) into four open triangular sectors:

| Sector | Condition | $u$ value |
|---|---|---|
| $R$ (right) | $\lvert x_2\rvert < x_1$ (so $x_1>0$) | $1-x_1$ |
| $L$ (left) | $\lvert x_2\rvert < -x_1$ (so $x_1<0$) | $1+x_1$ |
| $U$ (up) | $\lvert x_1\rvert < x_2$ (so $x_2>0$) | $1-x_2$ |
| $D$ (down) | $\lvert x_1\rvert < -x_2$ (so $x_2<0$) | $1+x_2$ |

The interfaces are the two diagonals $x_2 = x_1$ and $x_2 = -x_1$, which are straight (hence Lipschitz) curves. Each sector is an open triangle with Lipschitz boundary.

## Step 1: $u$ is continuous across the interfaces

Check each interface (take the open half of each diagonal lying in $\Omega$):

- **$x_2 = x_1$, $x_1>0$** (between $R$ and $U$): from $R$, $u = 1-x_1$; from $U$, $u = 1-x_2 = 1-x_1$. ✓
- **$x_2 = -x_1$, $x_1>0$** (between $R$ and $D$): from $R$, $u=1-x_1$; from $D$, $u = 1+x_2 = 1-x_1$. ✓
- **$x_2 = -x_1$, $x_1<0$** (between $L$ and $U$): from $L$, $u = 1+x_1$; from $U$, $u = 1-x_2 = 1+x_1$. ✓
- **$x_2 = x_1$, $x_1<0$** (between $L$ and $D$): from $L$, $u = 1+x_1$; from $D$, $u = 1+x_2 = 1+x_1$. ✓

So $u$ is continuous on all of $\Omega$.

## Step 2: The weak gradient exists and equals the piecewise classical gradient

On each sector $u$ is affine, with classical gradients

$$
\nabla u\big|_R = (-1,0),\quad \nabla u\big|_L = (1,0),\quad \nabla u\big|_U = (0,-1),\quad \nabla u\big|_D = (0,1).
$$

Define $g := \nabla u\big|_{\text{sector}}$ (arbitrary on the diagonals, a null set). We claim $g$ is the weak gradient of $u$.

Take any $\varphi \in C_c^\infty(\Omega)$. By the classical divergence theorem applied on each sector,

$$
\int_{\Omega} u\,\partial_{x_j}\varphi\,dx
= \sum_{S\in\{R,L,U,D\}} \int_S u\,\partial_{x_j}\varphi\,dx
= -\sum_S \int_S (\partial_{x_j} u)\,\varphi\,dx + \sum_S \int_{\partial S} u\,\varphi\,\nu_j^{(S)}\,dS.
$$

The boundary sum splits into internal interfaces (each shared by two sectors with opposite outward normals) and the part of $\partial S$ on $\partial\Omega$ (where $\varphi = 0$ by compact support). On every internal interface the two contributions cancel because $u$ is **continuous** (same trace from both sides) while the normals are opposite. Hence all boundary terms vanish and

$$
\int_{\Omega} u\,\partial_{x_j}\varphi\,dx = -\int_{\Omega} g_j\,\varphi\,dx,
$$

which is exactly the definition of $g_j$ being the weak derivative $\partial_{x_j} u$. So $u \in W^{1,1}_{\text{loc}}(\Omega)$ with weak gradient $g$.

## Step 3: Integrability of $u$ and $\nabla u$

**$u$ is bounded.** On $\Omega$, $\lvert x_1\rvert<1$ and $\lvert x_2\rvert<1$, so in every sector $\lvert u\rvert \le 2$. Since $\Omega$ is bounded, $u \in L^p(\Omega)$ for every $p \in [1,\infty]$.

**$\nabla u$ is bounded a.e.** From Step 2, $\lvert g\rvert = 1$ almost everywhere on $\Omega$ (the four gradient vectors all have unit length; the diagonals have measure zero). Therefore

$$
\|\nabla u\|_{L^p(\Omega)} = \lvert\Omega\rvert^{1/p} < \infty \quad \text{for } 1\le p < \infty,
$$

and $\|\nabla u\|_{L^\infty(\Omega)} = 1 < \infty$.

## Step 4: Conclusion

Combining Steps 2 and 3,

$$
\|u\|_{W^{1,p}(\Omega)} = \|u\|_{L^p(\Omega)} + \|\nabla u\|_{L^p(\Omega)} < \infty
$$

for every $p \in [1,\infty]$.

**The values of $p$ for which $u \in W^{1,p}(\Omega)$ are exactly $p \in [1,\infty]$, and the observation that the $W^{1,p}$ norm is finite for all $p$ is correct.**

The key mechanism: $u$ is globally continuous (the four affine pieces match on the diagonals), so the distributional derivative has no singular part; the piecewise gradient is constant of unit magnitude, hence in every $L^p$ on the bounded domain $\Omega$.

